  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how low-frequency radio signals from Earth can interact with the ionosphere and be detected by satellites overhead. A person on the ground is shown carrying a phone, which emits a red, wavy radio signal upward. In one case, the signal appears to travel through the ionosphere more directly; in another, it seems more distorted or spread out before reaching a satellite. Blue dots labeled as electrons fill the ionospheric region, emphasizing that this layer contains charged particles that affect radio-wave propagation.\n\nThe ionosphere is drawn as a broad atmospheric band above Earth, with approximate heights marked around 100 km and 500 km. Several satellites orbit above this region, suggesting space-based monitoring of these transmissions. The main message is that radio emissions from devices at Earth’s surface can leak upward into near-Earth space, where their path and shape are influenced by the electron-rich ionosphere. Overall, the image conveys the relationship between ground-based phone signals, ionospheric electrons, and satellite detection, highlighting how the ionosphere modifies radio communication as it travels from Earth toward space.",
